---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SITE_NAME_DISPLAY, CONTACT } from '../lib/brand.js';

/* Required, not decorative: the alerts plane stores email addresses, and a
   double opt-in flow without a privacy notice is an incomplete one. Kept short
   because a notice nobody reads protects nobody. */
const title = `Privacy — ${SITE_NAME_DISPLAY}`;
const description =
  `What ${SITE_NAME} stores, why, and how to have it deleted. Browsing is counted `
  + 'but never identified; the only personal data held is an email address you '
  + 'typed in yourself.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="page">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Bags</a> <span>/</span> <b>Privacy</b>
    </nav>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="note">
      Short version: the only personal data held is an email address you typed
      in yourself — into a price watch, or onto a brand request, where it is
      optional. Browsing is counted but not identified: what gets read and
      clicked is recorded as anonymous totals, and nothing ties those to you.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Browsing</h2>
      <p class="note">
        The catalog is static files. No account and no login. Your filter
        selections live in the URL, and your theme, your favourites and any
        saved filters live in your own browser's local storage — none of that
        reaches us.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Two things are counted, and it is worth being exact about them rather
        than calling the page anonymous and leaving it there. Vercel records a
        pageview for each page, without cookies and without an identifier that
        follows you between them. And we record which bags get looked at and
        which sellers get clicked through to, as counts: the bag, the seller
        and the time, and nothing about the person — no address, no IP, no
        cookie, no session, nothing joining one of those rows to another or to
        you. It is how we know which parts of the index are worth the work of
        keeping accurate.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        That ceiling is deliberate rather than an omission. Counting is enough
        to tell us what to measure next; knowing who did the counting is not
        worth asking you for, so we do not collect it and there is nothing
        held about your browsing for you to ask us to delete.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Price watches</h2>
      <table class="spectable">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">What we store</th><td>Your email address, what you asked to watch, and when.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Why</th><td>To email you when that price drops. Nothing else — no newsletter, no “related offers”.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Confirmation</th><td>Double opt-in. Nothing is ever sent to an address that has not clicked the confirmation link.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Delivery</th><td>We record what became of each email we send you — delivered, bounced, opened, or reported as spam — so we stop writing to addresses that are not working.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">How long</th><td>Until you unsubscribe. There is no other expiry.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Deletion</th><td>One click in any email. It deletes the row outright — the address is gone, not flagged as inactive — and takes the record of what we sent you with it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="note">
        Addresses are never written into the public repository this site is
        built from, and never appear in application logs; those record a
        numeric subscription id instead.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Brand requests</h2>
      <table class="spectable">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">What we store</th><td>The brand you named, the storefront link if you gave one, and your email address if you gave one. All three are what you typed; nothing is collected from you in the background.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Why</th><td>To decide what to crawl next. A brand asked for often is one we add sooner, and that ordering needs the requests counted rather than read once and deleted.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Your address</th><td>Optional, and not a subscription. It is there so we can answer you once, if the brand lands. It joins no list, gets no other mail, and is not the address a price watch uses.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Spam control</th><td>To stop one sender flooding the queue we store a one-way digest of your IP address mixed with today's date, and never the address itself. Because the date is inside it, the digest stops matching you at midnight UTC — it can count today's requests and cannot follow you across days.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">How long</th><td>Until the brand is added or ruled out, at which point the request has done its job.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Deletion</th><td>Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.privacy}`}>{CONTACT.privacy}</a> and it goes, address and all.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Who else sees it</h2>
      <p class="note">
        Three processors, each doing one job: <strong>Supabase</strong> hosts the
        database the address sits in, <strong>Resend</strong> delivers the
        email, and <strong>Vercel</strong> serves the site and counts the
        pageviews described above. Resend keeps its own delivery log containing
        recipient addresses, as every email provider does — so unsubscribing
        removes you from our side immediately,
        but their log retains a record of past sends for a period we do not
        control. No data is sold or shared with anyone else.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your rights, and how to use them</h2>
      <p class="note">
        You can ask what we hold about you, ask for it to be corrected, or ask
        for it to be deleted, and there is no charge for any of it. Unsubscribe
        already does the deletion instantly. For anything else, write to
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.privacy}`}>{CONTACT.privacy}</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Product data</h2>
      <p class="note">
        Specifications and prices come from brands' own published product
        feeds. Nothing on this site is estimated, and every value records where
        it came from. If you operate a store, see
        <a href="/bot/">about the crawler</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
